  "ideas": [
    {
      "title": "Sex-specific stochastic dynamic optimization of reproductive timing with recourse",
      "motivation": "The original query asks what drives reproduction in living systems. Evolutionary evidence shows that reproduction is driven by interacting processes: genetic variance in reproductive timing contributes to trait evolvability [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4416670776), assortative mating can change genetic variance and climate tracking [Author et al., 2021](https://openalex.org/W3141331266), sex-specific selection maps different traits to male and female fitness [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4366817449), and reproduction-survival trade-offs shape sex-biased longevity [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4412678672). The unresolved bottleneck is that these drivers are usually treated separately, so the field lacks a predictive framework that explains when genetic architecture, mating ecology, and life-history allocation jointly increase fitness versus when they create constraints. I propose to import uncertain and dynamic optimization with recourse from operations research [Author et al., 2013](https://openalex.org/W6997172968) and adapt it to reproduction: organisms make sequential, sex-specific decisions under uncertain mates, pollinators, climate, and mortality. The central claim is that dynamic recourse, not just a fixed reaction norm, is a mechanistic driver of reproductive success and evolvability.",
      "method_overview": "This method is intended to solve the lack of a predictive, sex-aware, environmentally contingent framework for reproduction; it can be divided into 4 parts: (1) a sex-specific life-history state space, (2) a stochastic dynamic optimization engine with recourse, (3) a genetic and mating-system module that tracks variance, and (4) a controlled simulation and validation protocol. Its primary hypothesis is that state-dependent dynamic recourse in reproductive timing causally increases lifetime reproductive success and additive genetic variance under environmental uncertainty, compared with fixed reaction norms, when all other factors are held constant.",
      "proposed_method": [
        {
          "name": "Sex-specific life-history state space",
          "description": "The model represents each individual by sex, age, condition, breeding value for reproductive timing, phenological state, gamete or offspring reserve, mating status, and survival hazard. Reproduction is decomposed into mate acquisition, gamete transfer or fertilization, offspring production, and survival, so that selection can be assigned to stages rather than conflated. Male and female fitness functions differ: male success is tied to mating opportunities and attractive or competitive traits, whereas female success is tied to fertility-linked traits and offspring production, consistent with sex-specific selection in dioecious insect-pollinated plants [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4366817449). Reproductive effort competes with survival, and the strength of this trade-off can differ between sexes, as predicted by asymmetric life-history models [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4412678672)."
        },
        {
          "name": "Stochastic dynamic optimization engine with recourse",
          "description": "At each time step, an individual chooses among beginning reproductive display or flowering, waiting, allocating resources to gametes, and investing in survival. The decision rule is solved using stochastic dynamic programming and scenario-based robust optimization, borrowed from operations research [Author et al., 2013](https://openalex.org/W6997172968). The value function maximizes expected lifetime genetic contribution subject to resource, mate-overlap, and survival constraints. Recourse allows the policy to update after partial observation of temperature, pollinator or mate availability, and mortality risk, creating an option value for delayed or spread reproduction. This directly addresses environmental change and phenological mismatch in reproductive timing [Author et al., 2021](https://openalex.org/W3141331266), and links timing variance to evolvability [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4416670776)."
        },
        {
          "name": "Genetic architecture and assortative mating module",
          "description": "Reproductive timing is modeled as a polygenic breeding value inherited with recombination and mutation. Mating requires temporal overlap, so the mating kernel implements assortative mating by phenological matching [Author et al., 2021](https://openalex.org/W3141331266). The module tracks additive genetic variance, linkage disequilibrium, polymorphism, adaptive lag, and sex-specific selection gradients. This allows the model to test whether dynamic recourse preserves evolvability [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4416670776) while avoiding the antagonistic variance effects previously associated with assortative mating [Author et al., 2021](https://openalex.org/W3141331266)."
        },
        {
          "name": "Controlled evaluation and empirical calibration protocol",
          "description": "The model is evaluated in an individual-based simulator calibrated to known sex-specific selection patterns [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4366817449) and sex-biased survival trade-offs [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4412678672). The key comparison is between dynamic recourse and fixed or open-loop policies under the same environmental scenarios. Outputs include lifetime reproductive success, mate number, offspring production, longevity, additive genetic variance, adaptive lag, and stage-specific selection gradients. The protocol includes randomized and unstructured policy controls to separate state-dependent organization from stochastic variability."
        }
