{
  "ideas": [
    {
      "title": "Evolvable reproductive recourse: sex-specific refractory delays and reserve banking as a mechanism linking genetic variance, mating ecology, and survival trade-offs",
      "motivation": "The original question asks what drives reproduction in living systems. Evidence from the subgraph shows that reproduction is driven by an interacting set of processes: heritable variance in reproductive timing supplies raw evolvability [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4416670776); assortative mating by timing can reshape genetic variance and adaptation under climate change [Author et al., 2021](https://openalex.org/W3141331266); sex-specific selection ties male fitness to mate attraction and female fitness to fertility-linked traits [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4366817449); and reproduction-survival trade-offs generate sex-biased longevity [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4412678672). The unresolved bottleneck is not another single trait but a predictive, sex-aware, environmentally contingent mechanism that determines when reproductive strategies increase fitness and evolvability versus when they impose constraints. To address this, the idea imports state-dependent threshold and refractory-period decision rules from behavioral ecology/economic trade-off modelling [Author et al., 2016](https://openalex.org/W2554280009), temporal optimization under environmental variation from sex-allocation theory [Author et al., 1986](https://openalex.org/W2029353030), and lifecycle-stage partitioning of sexual selection and conflict [Author et al., 2019](https://openalex.org/W2989981305). The central mechanism is not an external optimizer but heritable variation in recourse capacity itself: individuals evolve cue thresholds, delay limits, reserve-banking efficiency, and refractory costs. The primary hypothesis is that a heritable structured recourse mechanism—conditional reproductive delay coupled with sex-specific reserve banking—causally maintains higher long-term fitness by preserving additive genetic variance in reproductive timing when cues are unreliable and mate or pollinator overlap is low. This is not ordinary plasticity or bet-hedging because the decision is cue- and state-dependent, stores reproductive potential for future use, creates temporal niche partitioning among genotypes, and evolves under explicit sex-specific survival and fertility constraints.",
      "method_overview": "This method is intended to solve the lack of a unified, sex-aware, environmentally contingent mechanism for reproductive drivers; it can be divided into 4 parts: (1) an evolvable recourse trait complex that controls conditional delay, refractory recovery, and reserve banking; (2) a stochastic ecological-mating kernel that generates cue reliability, temporal mate overlap, and pollinator or mate limitation; (3) a sex-specific lifecycle fitness ledger that separates pre-mating, mating, post-mating, and survival components; and (4) controlled evolutionary simulations and a common-garden microcosm test that first isolate the recourse effect and then explore boundary conditions.",
      "proposed_method": [
        {
          "name": "Evolvable recourse trait complex",
          "description": "Represent recourse as a heritable mechanism, not an external optimizer. Individuals carry polygenic reaction norms for reproductive timing and for recourse parameters: cue threshold for reproduction, maximum conditional delay, refractory length, reserve conversion efficiency, cue-acquisition error, and sex-specific maintenance cost. The refractory/recovery state generalizes post-partum amenorrhea-like constraints, in which a reproductive event creates a temporary state that limits immediate future reproduction and couples current effort to future survival and fecundity [Author et al., 2016](https://openalex.org/W2554280009). Reserve banking stores deferred reproductive potential as female propagule or gamete reserves and male condition or mating-effort reserves, with decay and conversion rules. This creates temporal niche partitioning among genotypes and allows alleles to be expressed in later reproductive windows. The optimization template is a temporally varying sex-allocation problem, where the best allocation depends on environmental state and future expectations [Author et al., 1986](https://openalex.org/W2029353030). Because timing variance is heritable, the model can ask how recourse changes additive genetic variance and evolvability [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4416670776)."
        },
        {
          "name": "Stochastic ecological-mating kernel",
          "description": "Simulate fluctuating environmental optima for reproductive timing, imperfect cues, and mate or pollinator availability. Mating success depends on temporal overlap and assortative matching of reproductive timing, allowing nonrandom mating to inflate or depress genetic variance depending on environmental change [Author et al., 2021](https://openalex.org/W3141331266). Mate acquisition and attractive traits are sex-specific, so male fitness can depend more on flowering duration or display traits while female fitness depends more on fertility-linked traits, as observed in dioecious insect-pollinated plants [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4366817449). The kernel can be parameterized for plants, animals, or digital organisms by changing the mapping from cue to mate availability and from display trait to mating success."
        },
        {
          "name": "Sex-specific lifecycle fitness ledger",
          "description": "Partition fitness into lifecycle stages: survival to reproduction, mate acquisition, gamete transfer or fertilization, offspring production, and post-reproductive survival. This follows lifecycle-explicit approaches to sexual selection and sexual conflict, allowing genetic load and sexually antagonistic selection to be quantified rather than collapsed into a single fitness measure [Author et al., 2019](https://openalex.org/W2989981305). The ledger includes asymmetric trade-offs: female offspring production can constrain both sexes, whereas male mating effort can impose weaker indirect fertility constraints, producing sex-biased longevity under monogamy and other mating systems [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4412678672). Sex-specific selection gradients on display and fertility traits are estimated from the ledger, linking Bateman-style patterns to life-history outcomes [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4366817449)."
        },
        {
          "name": "Threshold and evolvability analysis",
          "description": "Use analytic invasion conditions and individual-based evolution to identify when recourse invades and when it is disfavored. The key predicted thresholds are critical cue reliability and critical mate-overlap levels below which structured recourse is favored and above which fixed or simple plastic strategies win. Outputs include additive genetic variance in reproductive timing, adaptive lag, mean fitness, sex-specific longevity, genetic load, and extinction risk. This connects directly to the role of genetic variance in trait evolvability [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4416670776) and to the environmental dependence of variance under assortative mating [Author et al., 2021](https://openalex.org/W3141331266)."
        }
      ],
      "analogy_mapping": [
        {
          "inspiration": "A Behavioral Ecology Approach to Trade-offs in Reproduction: Modelling Women’s Reproductive Strategies and Empirical Studies of Post-partum Amenorrhea",
          "concept": "state-dependent reproductive decision rules and refractory/recovery constraints such as post-partum amenorrhea",
