        "Phase 1 primary hypothesis test: vary only one factor, structured recourse ON versus OFF. In ON, individuals can conditionally delay reproduction and bank reserves according to cue and state. In OFF, individuals perceive the same cues but cannot delay or bank; they either reproduce at the scheduled time or lose that reproductive opportunity. Run at least 200 replicates per treatment in a single challenging environment with unreliable cues and limited mate overlap. Primary predictions: ON populations have higher long-term mean fitness and retain higher additive genetic variance in reproductive timing than OFF populations.",
        "Control panel to attribute effect to structured recourse: (a) fixed timing, (b) cue-plastic timing without banking, (c) delay-only without banking, (d) banking-only without conditional delay, and (e) randomized recourse with the same average delay and banking rates but assigned independently of cues and state. The randomized control provides the unstructured mixture condition, while delay-only and banking-only test each active component alone.",
        "Confounder exclusion in Phase 1: set direct recourse costs to zero; use identical cue information across cue-using treatments; hold density dependence constant; measure effective population size and neutral marker heterozygosity to rule out drift; include a forced-skip treatment that skips reproduction but does not bank to test whether any advantage is simply increased adult survival; compare age-specific survival and fecundity to rule out demographic rescue.",
        "Phase 2 one-factor-at-a-time boundary tests: starting from the Phase 1 baseline, vary cue reliability alone, mate overlap alone, environmental autocorrelation alone, extrinsic mortality alone, recourse cost alone, and sex-specific trade-off strength alone. Estimate critical thresholds where recourse stops being favored. Predicted pattern: recourse is favored below critical cue reliability and mate overlap, but high reliability or high overlap removes its advantage.",
